Change(genesis='e30427e699992327267add669c3bc989f6d16525ba6f493121eec7778638e0ce', deps=('0f540920fb2e8da324afd9f7044e2830bfd6c69151fb596bc3d4d147615b77c9',), event=Authored(todo='coreface', at=datetime(2026, 11, 25, 18, 4, 48, 543342), actor='bmabsout', kind='todo', created=datetime(2026, 11, 22, 0, 0, 0), body="What a unit must read to use the store library falls from about 140 thousand tokens to what its task needs: the library's public face is cut to what the code and its specification do not already say, what no consumer uses stops being public, and the core divides into crates by its own layers, so that a unit that only builds and reads events reads one small crate. The application then depends on the crates it uses.", spec=Flat(value=0.38), rationale=("Every unit of the application is charged the whole face of the library's", 'core before it reads a line of its own: it is the largest single term in', "each unit's reading cost, and the owner's aim is that a change needs", 'little context.'), category='', waiting_on='', detail="State on 2026-11-22: in work in the library's repository as a measured note and its first stage (the public items' doc comments, words only).\n\nState on 2026-11-22: the first stage is merged in the library: its public doc comments are cut to what the code and the specification do not say, with no code changed, and the library's face by this repository's measure falls from 140 to 112 thousand tokens. The next stage, in work: the data a store holds becomes its own crate, which a unit that only builds and reads events depends on alone. This repository takes the gain when it pins that revision and names the smaller crate in such units.\n\nState on 2026-11-22: the library's data crate is merged: a program that only builds and reads events reads about 22 thousand tokens in place of 113. In work here: this repository pins that revision, and each unit names the smallest crate it needs.\n\nState on 2026-11-22: release 2.1.219 pins the library's data crate: four units depend on it alone, and every other unit is charged about 115 thousand tokens for the library in place of 140; the measures over budget fall from 17 to 15. The library's next stage is merged too (the time conversions in the data crate, its face shorter), and the pin that frees four more units is in work.\n\nState on 2026-11-23: release 2.1.225 pins the library where the time conversions are in the data crate: mail, triage and conversations depend on the data crate alone (each about 93 thousand tokens cheaper to change), and the data crate measures 21.9 thousand tokens by this repository's tool, under the budget. What holds the other units to the core is the readings, the policy and the declared schemas: the library's next stages.\n\nState on 2026-11-24: the library's next stage is merged: the caches, the readings (the graph of events, the registers, the policy trait and the proof of a signature) and the declared schemas are three crates beside the data crate, each under the budget; a program that reads an entity's registers under a policy reads about 42 thousand tokens of the library where it read 111. In work here: this repository pins that revision and each unit names the smallest set of those crates it needs.\n\n2026-11-24, release 2.1.248: the store library is pinned at the revision where its caches, its readings and its declared schemas are crates of their own. Nine units no longer depend on the library's core and name only the small crates they use; nineteen still do. What holds them, by the count of units held: the store and its doors (ten), the evaluator's terms (ten), the todo schema's events and view (eleven, none alone), tags (one), the digest (one). The next stage of the library follows that order, the store first.\n\n2026-11-24, release 2.1.252, deployed: the store library is pinned at the revision where its store, tag sets, nests and summary tree are crates of their own. Eight more units name only the small crates they use; eleven still depend on the core, held by the evaluator's terms, the todo schema's events and view, and the todo's readings: the library's next stages.\n\nIn work on 2026-11-25: the library's next stage: the terms as data apart from the evaluator, the evaluator in its crate, and the todo schema's events with them if their edges allow it. Eleven units still read the core, ten of them for a term or the evaluator.\n\nState on 2026-11-25: the library's terms, evaluator and todo schema are crates of their own (its revision dc41c12): a program that writes a todo with a decaying price reads about 32 thousand tokens of the library in place of 113 thousand, and what is left of the core is 17 thousand, under the budget. In work: the readings of a todo in their crate, so the core is only a facade. The application adopts these revisions after the memory digest's stage in flight lands, since one of them changes the summary view's call.\n\nState on 2026-11-25, later: the library's readings of a todo are a crate of their own (its revision ed70e10): a program that reads a todo's price and its row in a list reads about 64 thousand tokens of the library in place of 114 thousand and no store code, and the library's core is a facade over its crates. Left for the library: a review of what is public across the new crates, and a release. Left here: the application's units on the smallest set of those crates, after the memory digest's stage in flight lands.\n\nState on 2026-11-25, last: the application is pinned at the library's revision ed70e10 (release 2.1.259) and no unit depends on the library's core any more (twelve did): each names every item at the crate that defines it and is charged 50 to 80 thousand tokens of the library where it was charged 114 thousand. The summary view's new call is taken with the one water that gives the earlier view, so no summary became owed. Left: the library's review of what is public and its release; here, the two-water view for the provider's cache.\n\nIn work on 2026-11-25: the library's review of what each of its crates makes public, item by item.", source=None, subtodos=(), notes=(), note=''))